flusher: leave a stamp after a flush that sent awards, for the OS to read

An OS that integrates the service natively wants to tell the player, on a
surface of its own, that the awards earned without a connection have now
reached RetroAchievements. The service knows exactly when that happens --
flush_pending_awards logs "Flush complete: ... flushed=N" -- but a log is not
a signal another process can consume: it rotates, it holds every request
line, and reading it back for one event is guesswork.

So, after a flush that sent at least one award, write <CONFIG_DIR>/last-flush
holding one line, "<epoch> <flushed>", whole or not at all (a temp file and
os.replace, as es_export does for cached_game_ids.txt). A flush that sent
nothing writes nothing: a stamp means awards went. The reader removes the
file once it has said so, which is what makes the message fire once per
flush rather than once per look. Nothing in the service reads it.

Written for ROCKNIX (fork issue maxengel/rocknix#173), whose EmulationStation
reads and clears the stamp when its next sync card runs: "OFFLINE
ACHIEVEMENTS HAVE BEEN SENT TO RETROACHIEVEMENTS." Offered upstream as-is
(ROCKNIX D-RA-002: whatever the fork changes in the proxy goes back); the
same file would serve the KNULLI integration, which already reads
cached_game_ids.txt.

